        "url": "https://9to5mac.com/2026/05/22/ios-26-iphone-always-on-display-wallpaper-blur/",
        "content_text": "<div class=\"feat-image\"><img src=\"https://9to5mac.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/6/2025/08/ios-26-clock-lock-screen-b6.jpg?quality=82&#038;strip=all&#038;w=1600\" /></div><p>Apple’s Always On Display has been fairly consistent since it debuted on iPhone 14 Pro. With iOS 26, Apple quietly changed how it works in a way that may not be obvious.</p>\n\n\n\n <a data-layer-pagetype=\"post\" data-layer-postcategory=\"ios,ios-26,iphone\" data-layer-viewtype=\"unknown\" data-post-id=\"1014532\" href=\"https://9to5mac.com/2026/05/22/ios-26-iphone-always-on-display-wallpaper-blur/#more-1014532\" class=\"more-link\">more…</a>",
